NNAAMMEE
     uueeffiissiiggnn — UEFI Secure Boot signing utility

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     uueeffiissiiggnn --kk _k_e_y --cc _c_e_r_t_i_f_i_c_a_t_e --oo _o_u_t_p_u_t [--vv] _f_i_l_e
     uueeffiissiiggnn --VV [--vv] _f_i_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uueeffiissiiggnn utility signs PE binary files using Authenticode scheme, as
     required by UEFI Secure Boot specification.  Alternatively, it can be
     used to view and verify existing signatures.  These options are avail‐
     able:

     --VV  Determine whether the file is signed.  Note that this does not verify
         the correctness of the signature; only that the file contains a sig‐
         nature.

     --kk  Name of file containing the private key used to sign the binary.

     --cc  Name of file containing the certificate used to sign the binary.

     --oo  Name of file to write the signed binary to.

     --vv  Be verbose.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The uueeffiissiiggnn utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Generate self-signed certificate and use it to sign a binary:
           /usr/share/examples/uefisign/uefikeys testcert
           uefisign -c testcert.pem -k testcert.key -o signed-binary binary

     View signature:
           uefisign -Vv binary

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     openssl(1), loader(8), uefi(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The uueeffiissiiggnn command appeared in FreeBSD 10.2.

